                            <comment id="503723" author="adilger" created="Fri, 16 Oct 2026 01:02:25 +0000">&lt;p&gt;Thinking about this further, we could infer the approximate offset of holes in a missing object from holes in the EC parity stripes, since the parity will also be sparse if all of the data stripes for a row are holes. That would put a bound on the start/end of holes for missing objects to be within &lt;tt&gt;k &amp;#42; stripe_size&lt;/tt&gt; or so. That could potentially make a huge difference for large sparse files since at most a few extra MiB would be copied per hole. It might be enough to do &lt;tt&gt;SEEK_HOLE&lt;/tt&gt; and &lt;tt&gt;SEEK_DATA&lt;/tt&gt; on the sparse EC parity stripes, then align the offsets up or down (depending on data or hole) to the start of the raidset row.  &lt;/p&gt;</comment>
